{% set local_problem %}
<p>Madison homeowners: if your house went up between 2000 and 2015, your gutters were probably the cheapest option your builder could find. That's not a knock on builders — it's just how spec housing works. They buy gutters by the truckload and install whatever meets code.</p>

<p>The result? Thousands of Madison homes running 5-inch sectional gutters, .027 gauge aluminum, hangers at the code-minimum 32 inches. For the first five years, everything seems fine. Then seams start separating. Hangers pull loose after an ice load. And every time a spring storm dumps 2 inches in an hour, the gutters can't keep up.</p>

<p>We've replaced builder-grade gutters across Madison subdivisions for 15+ years — <a href="/recent-projects/">Clift's Cove, Bradford Creek, Mill Creek, Dublin Park area</a>, the neighborhoods along Highway 72. Same pattern everywhere: homes hit the 7-10 year mark and the gutters start failing.</p>

<p><strong>Here's something most people don't realize about Madison:</strong> Subdivision lots are graded to drain into each other and toward retention ponds. Your property might be receiving runoff from 2-3 neighbors uphill. Your gutters need to handle your roof water <em>plus</em> whatever the soil is already dealing with. Undersized gutters just make the problem worse.</p>

<p><strong>Our opinion:</strong> If your builder-grade gutters are 8+ years old and you're seeing any overflow, replace the whole system. Patching seams is a temporary fix that usually fails within 2-3 years. The cost difference between patching and replacing isn't enough to justify the hassle of doing it twice.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Seamless gutters are fabricated from a continuous roll of aluminum coil — formed on-site by a portable machine we bring to your driveway. Instead of pre-cut 10-foot sections joined with seams, you get one continuous piece per run. Connections happen only at corners and downspout outlets.</p>

<p><strong>What we actually install:</strong> 6-inch K-style gutters in .032 gauge aluminum. Hidden hangers at 24-inch intervals (tighter than the 32-inch code minimum). 3x4 downspouts positioned every 35-40 linear feet. Everything measured and cut to your home's exact dimensions — no standard sizes.</p>

<p><strong>Why those specs matter:</strong> Six-inch gutters hold about 40% more water than five-inch. The .032 gauge is noticeably stiffer than .027 — it doesn't dent when a ladder bumps it, and it holds shape better over 20+ years. And 24-inch hanger spacing? That prevents the sagging you see on older systems where hangers are 32 inches apart.</p>

<p><strong>The honest bit:</strong> "Seamless" is a bit of marketing. You still have seams at inside corners, outside corners, and downspout drops. What you don't have is a seam every 10 feet in the middle of a run. Those mid-run seams are what fail first — they expand and contract differently than the gutter body, and eventually the caulk lets go.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We're just 10 miles away in Huntsville — not a franchise from out of state, not a crew passing through town looking for work. We've served Madison County for over 20 years, which means we watched these subdivisions go up, saw the builder-grade problems develop, and have replaced hundreds of failing systems.</p>

<p>We know the difference between <a href="/recent-projects/">Clift's Cove drainage challenges and Bradford Creek's grading</a>. We understand why Madison's subdivision layout creates specific problems. And we're close enough to respond fast when you need us.</p>

<p><strong>Warning from experience:</strong> Silicon is not a gutter sealer. We see it all the time — someone tried to patch a seam with bathroom caulk. Doesn't work. Water breaks it down within a year. If you're getting a quote from someone and they mention silicon, find another contractor.</p>

<p><strong>See our work:</strong> Check out our <a href="/recent-projects/">recent projects page</a> to see gutter installations we've done around Madison.</p>
{% endset %}

